---
/**
 * NotSection — "What Basset is not." section.
 *
 * Recreates design/README.md §7 (What Basset is not) and the approved
 * reference in design/design-reference/Basset Landing.dc.html exactly: a
 * left-aligned section with **no kicker** (per the brief) whose sole body
 * is the H2 followed by a three-column grid of mono-tag + muted-body
 * clarifications. The section is separated from FailureModes above it by
 * a 1px hairline top border — the global rule the brief specifies for
 * section separation.
 *
 * Column tags are verbatim per the brief: "not a log viewer", "not a
 * cloud service", "not an AI oracle". Bodies follow verbatim beneath.
 *
 * The three-column grid uses `minmax(min(240px, 100%), 1fr)` instead of
 * the raw `minmax(240px, 1fr)` the brief cites. The natural three -> two
 * -> one collapse the brief calls for is preserved at every wider width
 * (the min() resolves to 240px whenever the container has room), but the
 * min() lets each track shrink below 240px when the container is
 * narrower than that (e.g. a 320px viewport whose content column is only
 * 280px after --content-padding-x), which prevents the horizontal-scroll
 * overflow the raw form would cause at the narrow end of the range.
 *
 * No screenshots or product imagery — this section is text-only per the
 * brief.
 *
 * Every color, size, spacing, radius, and copy string here is bound by the
 * brief; values live in src/styles/tokens.css and are read through the
 * token custom properties so a single change ripples across the site. QA
 * verifies against design/README.md §7 at 1440x900 (three columns),
 * ~900x wide (two columns), and 390x844 (one column), and confirms there
 * is no horizontal scroll at 320px.
 */
---

<section class="not-section" aria-labelledby="not-section-heading">
  <h2 id="not-section-heading" class="not-heading">What Basset is not.</h2>

  <div class="columns">
    <div class="col">
      <span class="tag">not a log viewer</span>
      <p class="body">It starts where viewers stop.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
      <span class="tag">not a cloud service</span>
      <p class="body">
        It runs where you decide, and your source and logs stay under your control.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
      <span class="tag">not an AI oracle</span>
      <p class="body">
        Deterministic evidence first, any AI sits on top to explain, never to invent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Section container — the brief's global separation rule: a 1px hairline
     top border between sections, with fluid vertical padding. Left-aligned
     block, matching every other section. No kicker on this section per
     the brief. */
  .not-section {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    padding: var(--section-padding-y) 0;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* Section H2 — the global h2 rule in BaseLayout already applies the type
     tokens (family/size/weight/line-height/letter-spacing); we reset the
     top margin to 0 so the section padding is the sole source of vertical
     rhythm above it, and pin ink color defensively so a global tweak
     cannot mute it. */
  .not-heading {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Column grid — auto-fit + minmax(min(240px, 100%), 1fr) collapses
     three columns to two and then to one as the viewport narrows, per
     the brief. The min(240px, 100%) idiom lets each track shrink below
     240px when the container is narrower than that (e.g. a 320px viewport
     whose content column is only 280px after --content-padding-x), which
     prevents horizontal overflow at the narrow end of the range. At every
     wider width the behaviour is unchanged — minmax resolves to
     minmax(240px, 1fr). */
  .columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(240px, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: clamp(24px, 3vw, 40px);
  }

  /* Column tag — mono 13px 600 ink on the neutral tag fill, with 5px 10px
     padding and the 6px tag radius per the brief. display: inline-block
     lets the tag hug its own text width instead of stretching to the
     column. */
  .tag {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: var(--color-tag-fill);
    padding: 5px 10px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-tag);
  }

  /* Column body — 16px/26px muted per the brief and AC #4, sitting 12px
     below the tag. margin: 12px 0 0 resets the browser's default
     paragraph margins so the column's own rhythm is the sole source of
     separation. */
  .body {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--font-size-body);
    line-height: var(--line-height-body);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
